window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][37] = "<h1>Part VII — Technology and AI</h1>\n<span id=\"c21\"></span>\n<h2>C21 — How Products Work: Technical Fluency</h2>\n<span id=\"c21-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>&quot;What happens when you open this app and load your feed?&quot; &quot;Explain to a stakeholder what an API is.&quot; &quot;Should voice requests be processed on the device or in the cloud?&quot; &quot;The page is slow. How do you work with engineers to find out why?&quot; These are not coding questions. They test whether you can talk with engineers as a credible partner, reason about feasibility, cost and risk, and translate technical constraints into user outcomes. An associate PM is not expected to build systems. They are expected to know the shape of one, to ask questions that make engineers' answers more useful, and not to propose things that cannot work.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code> from the round structures candidates commonly report: technical-fluency questions at Google, Meta, Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Netflix, Stripe and others, ranging from short &quot;explain this concept&quot; prompts to scenario questions inside product and execution rounds, with more depth for technical-leaning roles <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. On the job it is the daily work of reading design documents, joining reviews, asking about estimates and risks, and noticing when a request has a hidden cost.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> Whether your technical understanding is accurate, practical and tied to users <code>Inferred</code>. Interviewers listen for a correct picture of a request's path; the ability to explain a concept simply without being wrong; sensible questions to engineers; awareness that choices (cache, queue, on-device processing) trade latency, cost, freshness, privacy and complexity; respect for reliability and security; and the discipline to say what you do not know.</p>\n<p>Six observable behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You describe what happens between a user action and the result, hop by hop, and say where time and failures concentrate.</li>\n<li>You explain APIs, data models, databases and pipelines accurately in plain language.</li>\n<li>You name the tradeoffs of common architecture choices (caching, queues, services, on-device versus cloud).</li>\n<li>You reason about performance with percentiles and budgets, and about reliability with availability targets and dependencies.</li>\n<li>You cover security and privacy basics, including what data leaves the device and why.</li>\n<li>You ask engineers focused questions, and you estimate and read design documents with an eye to risk.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Six patterns end technical-fluency answers: jargon used incorrectly, an answer that ignores the user, a &quot;just cache it&quot; with no thought of staleness, &quot;it should be fast&quot; with no number, treating security as someone else's job, and bluffing past the edge of your knowledge.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c21-c21-1-why-technical-fluency-matters-and-what-it-is-not\"></span>\n<h4>C21.1 Why technical fluency matters, and what it is not</h4>\n<p>Technical fluency is enough understanding of how software systems work to reason about what is feasible, what it costs, where it breaks, and what questions to ask. It is not the ability to write production code. A PM who lacks it makes three kinds of mistake: proposing features whose cost is hidden (a real-time feature that needs a new data pipeline), accepting engineering answers without testing them (&quot;it's complicated&quot;), and missing risks that are visible to anyone who knows the shape of the system (a single point of failure, a privacy leak).</p>\n<p><strong>What to learn, and in what order.</strong> The request path (what happens between a tap and a result), APIs and data models, how data is stored and read, the common architecture patterns and their tradeoffs, how code reaches users, performance and reliability, security and privacy, and the platform differences between mobile and web. Each section of this chapter takes one.</p>\n<p><strong>Limits.</strong> Depth beyond this belongs to engineers. A senior PM at a deeply technical product needs more; the Volume 2 backlog holds that material. Do not bluff: &quot;I'm not certain how the database handles that; I'd ask the engineer what the failure mode is&quot; is a stronger answer than a confident wrong one.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> Asked how to speed up a slow report, a candidate says: &quot;Just cache it.&quot; The interviewer asks what happens when the underlying data changes. The candidate has not considered that a cache stores a copy that can go stale, so the report may show old numbers, and that the right answer depends on how fresh the data must be and how often it changes (C21.5). Fluency is not knowing the word &quot;cache&quot;; it is knowing what it costs.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hidden risks.</strong> Two. <em>False confidence:</em> a half-understood term used in a meeting loses engineers' trust faster than silence. <em>Over-reach:</em> telling engineers how to build instead of what must be true (C16); fluency serves better questions, not better instructions.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-c21-2-a-request-end-to-end-client-network-server-data-store\"></span>\n<h4>C21.2 A request end to end: client, network, server, data store</h4>\n<p>Almost every product interaction follows the same path (FW-C21-01). A user acts on a <strong>client</strong> (a phone app or a browser). The client needs the address of the server, found through the Domain Name System (DNS), which turns a name such as <code>example.com</code> into a numeric address. The client opens a connection across the network, usually secured so others cannot read it. The request arrives at a front door (a load balancer that spreads requests among servers, often behind a content delivery network that serves common files from a location near the user). An application server runs the logic. It reads or writes a <strong>data store</strong> (a database), may call other internal services or outside partners (payments, maps), builds a response, and sends it back. The client then renders what it received.</p>\n<p><strong>Latency.</strong> <strong>Latency</strong> is the time between a request and its response. The user experiences the sum of every hop: name lookup, connection setup, network travel, server processing, database work, transfer of the response, and rendering on the device. Which hop dominates varies: on a slow mobile network it is often transfer and connection setup; on a fast network with a heavy page it may be rendering; on a poorly written query it is the database. <strong>Throughput</strong> is the amount of work a system completes per unit of time (requests per second); latency and throughput are different, and improving one does not guarantee the other.</p>\n<p><strong>Percentiles, not averages.</strong> The average hides the slow tail that real users feel. The 50th percentile (median) is the typical experience; the 95th or 99th percentile is what the unluckiest few percent see (C12). A page with a 1.0-second median and a 6-second 95th percentile is unpleasant for one user in twenty, and at scale that is many people. Always ask for the percentile.</p>\n<p><strong>What can fail at each hop.</strong> The name lookup (the name does not resolve), the connection (timeouts, poor networks), the server (overloaded, a bug), the database (slow query, lock, outage), a dependency (a partner is down), and the client (an old version, a low-end device). A PM's useful question at each hop is what happens to the user when it fails, and whether the system degrades gracefully or breaks entirely. CS-C21-02 in section 10 shows a failure in the name-lookup and routing hop that took a very large service off the internet.</p>\n<p><strong>Human thresholds.</strong> A response under about 0.1 seconds feels instantaneous, about 1 second keeps the user's flow of thought, and around 10 seconds loses attention (Nielsen's long-standing guidance <code>[V: Nielsen, Usability Engineering, 1993; Miller, 1968]</code>). These are rules of thumb, not laws; measure your own users' abandonment against load time.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> A map of the path locates problems and converts &quot;it's slow&quot; into &quot;which hop?&quot; <strong>When not to draw it in full.</strong> For a small question (a text change) it is overkill; use it when time, failure or cost is the issue.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-c21-3-apis-and-data-models\"></span>\n<h4>C21.3 APIs and data models</h4>\n<p>An <strong>API</strong> (application programming interface) is a defined way for one piece of software to request data or actions from another. The client calls the server's API (&quot;give me this user's orders&quot;); a payments partner exposes an API that your server calls (&quot;charge this card&quot;); a platform offers APIs that outside developers build on (C19). An API is a contract: it states what can be asked, what comes back, and what errors mean. Contracts are hard to change once others depend on them, which is why versioning and deprecation matter (C19.3).</p>\n<p><strong>The words you will hear.</strong> An <em>endpoint</em> is one address of the API (for example, &quot;get order&quot;). A request has a <em>method</em> (read, create, update, delete), parameters, and often an authentication token; a response has a status (success, client error, server error) and a body. <em>Rate limits</em> cap how many calls a client may make, to protect the service. Many web APIs follow a common style (REST) in which resources have addresses and methods act on them; others let the client ask for exactly the fields it needs (GraphQL). You need not know the differences in detail; you need to ask what the contract is and who depends on it.</p>\n<p><strong>Idempotency.</strong> An operation is <strong>idempotent</strong> if doing it more than once has the same effect as doing it once. Reading a record is idempotent; charging a card is not, unless designed to be. Networks fail, and clients retry; if a retry of &quot;charge $50&quot; arrives twice, the customer may be charged $100. The standard defense is an <em>idempotency key</em>: the client sends a unique token with the request, and the server remembers it, so a repeat returns the first result instead of repeating the action. For any product that moves money, sends messages or creates orders, ask: what happens if this request is sent twice? (C17's payments checklist, EX-C17-06, requires idempotent sends for the same reason.)</p>\n<p><strong>Data models and schemas.</strong> A <strong>schema</strong> is the defined structure of stored data: the entities (users, orders, products), their fields, and how they relate. A data model is how the world is represented: one order has many items; a user has many orders; an item belongs to many categories. Decisions in the model are hard to reverse: if a &quot;user&quot; can have only one address, supporting several addresses later means a migration. Questions to ask: what are the entities, what is unique, what can be many, what must never be lost, and how will this change in a year?</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Treating the API and schema as contracts focuses attention on what is hard to change. <strong>When not to go deep.</strong> For a new internal prototype, a rough model is fine; for anything others will build on or that holds money or personal data, the contract deserves care.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-c21-4-databases-and-data-pipelines-reading-a-sql-query\"></span>\n<h4>C21.4 Databases and data pipelines; reading a SQL query</h4>\n<p>A database stores data and answers questions about it. A <em>relational</em> database keeps data in tables of rows and columns, and queries are written in SQL. Other kinds (key-value stores, document stores, search indexes) trade the flexibility of relational queries for speed or scale in particular uses. PM-level fluency is knowing what a table holds, what a query returns, and why a query is slow. This section is about reading a query; C13.6a teaches writing the queries that check a metric, on the same kind of data.</p>\n<p><strong>Why queries are slow, and indexes.</strong> A query that must examine every row of a table (a full scan) takes time proportional to the table's size. A <strong>database index</strong> is a data structure that lets the database find rows by a column's value without scanning the whole table, as an index in a book lets you find a topic without reading every page. An index speeds reads on that column and costs space and some write time. CS-C21-01 shows a missing index turning a 2.1-second query into a 12-millisecond one.</p>\n<p><strong>Reading a SQL query (FW-C21-04).</strong> You will see queries written by analysts and engineers, and you should be able to read them and test their claims. A query has a few parts: what to return (<code>SELECT</code>), from which table (<code>FROM</code>), how tables connect (<code>JOIN ... ON</code>), which rows to keep (<code>WHERE</code>), how to summarize (<code>GROUP BY</code> with <code>SUM</code>, <code>COUNT</code>, <code>AVG</code>), and how to order or limit the result. Ask of any query: What is one row of each table (the <em>grain</em>)? What does the join do to the row count? What is filtered out, and are <code>NULL</code> values handled? What time zone are dates in? Does the total match a number you already know?</p>\n<p>The commonest trap is a <em>fan-out</em>: joining a table of orders to a table of order items repeats each order once per item, so summing the order amount counts it several times. Worked example 2 reads one such query.</p>\n<p><strong>Data pipelines.</strong> A <strong>data pipeline</strong> is a set of automated steps that moves data from where it is produced to where it is analyzed, typically collecting events, cleaning and transforming them, and loading them into a store for reporting. Pipelines run in <em>batches</em> (for example, nightly) or as <em>streams</em> (continuously). They matter to a PM because dashboards and experiments depend on them: a late or broken pipeline makes a metric look like it moved (C13), a definition changed in the pipeline changes the metric silently, and the &quot;freshness&quot; of data limits what decisions are possible (a nightly batch cannot support a real-time alert). Ask: where does this number come from, how fresh is it, and who owns the transformation?</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Knowing the grain and the join behavior catches the most common data errors. <strong>When not to read the query yourself.</strong> For a high-stakes number, have the analyst walk you through it and reproduce a known total.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-c21-5-architecture-at-pm-depth-services-caching-queues\"></span>\n<h4>C21.5 Architecture at PM depth: services, caching, queues</h4>\n<p>Architecture is how a system is divided into parts and how they communicate. Three patterns come up constantly (FW-C21-05).</p>\n<p><strong>Monolith and services.</strong> A <em>monolith</em> is a single deployable program that does everything; <em>services</em> (often called microservices when small) are separate programs, each owning part of the work, that call one another over a network. Services let teams work and release independently and scale parts separately; they add network calls, more places to fail, and the need to coordinate changes across teams. Neither is better in general: small teams often do best with a monolith, large organizations with many teams often need services. A PM's question is not &quot;which?&quot; but &quot;what does this boundary cost us, and who owns each side?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Caching.</strong> A <strong>cache</strong> is a stored copy of data kept close to where it is needed so that it can be served faster than recomputing or refetching it. Caches exist at many levels (the browser, a content delivery network, the application, the database). A cache improves latency and reduces load, at the cost of <em>staleness</em> (the copy may be out of date) and complexity (deciding when to refresh or remove entries). The central tradeoff: how fresh must this data be? A price shown on a product page may tolerate a few minutes of staleness; an account balance may not. The effect on speed is computed in Worked example 3: average latency depends on the <em>hit rate</em> (the share of requests served from the cache), but the slow tail comes from misses.</p>\n<p><strong>Queues and asynchronous work.</strong> A <strong>message queue</strong> holds tasks or messages until a worker processes them, so that the part of the system that receives a request need not wait for slow work. Sending a confirmation email, resizing an uploaded photo or updating a recommendation can be done asynchronously: the user gets a quick response and the work finishes shortly after. The costs: the result is not immediate (the user may need to see &quot;processing&quot;), failures must be retried safely (idempotency again), and ordering and duplication become design questions. Queues also absorb bursts: a spike of requests is stored and worked through, instead of overwhelming the system.</p>\n<p><strong>Synchronous and asynchronous; consistency.</strong> In a synchronous design the caller waits for the result; in an asynchronous one it does not. When data is copied across places (caches, replicas, services), copies can briefly disagree: <em>eventual consistency</em> means they will agree given a little time. Users notice when an action they just took is not reflected (&quot;I saved it and it's gone&quot;), so the product must either hide the delay or accept it deliberately.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Naming what each pattern buys and costs lets you take part in a design discussion. <strong>When not to add them.</strong> Each adds moving parts; &quot;keep it simple until measurement says otherwise&quot; is usually right for a new product.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-c21-6-cloud-deployment-pipelines-environments\"></span>\n<h4>C21.6 Cloud, deployment pipelines, environments</h4>\n<p>Most products run on rented computing in data centers operated by cloud providers, paid for by use. This turns infrastructure from a fixed cost into a variable one, and puts a <em>cost per request</em> on every feature. A PM who knows that a new recommendation call costs a fraction of a cent per request, and that the product serves a billion requests a day, will see that a fraction of a cent is a very large number (Worked example 4). Ask engineers for the cost to run a feature at expected volume, not only the cost to build it.</p>\n<p><strong>Environments and deployment.</strong> Code moves through <em>environments</em>: engineers' own machines, a shared <em>staging</em> environment that mimics production for testing, and <em>production</em>, where users are. A <em>deployment pipeline</em> is the automated route that builds the code, runs tests and releases it; teams that release often use automated tests and flags (C17.2) to make each release small and reversible. What matters to a PM: how long a change takes to reach users (hours or weeks), what can be rolled back and how fast (C17.5), and what differs between staging and production (a difference is where surprises hide).</p>\n<p><strong>Regions and failure.</strong> Providers operate in many regions and zones; a service run in one location fails if that location fails, and running in several adds cost and complexity. How much redundancy is justified depends on what an outage costs (C21.7).</p>\n<span id=\"c21-c21-7-performance-scalability-reliability\"></span>\n<h4>C21.7 Performance, scalability, reliability</h4>\n<p><strong>Performance</strong> is how fast the system responds (latency) and how much it handles (throughput). <strong>Scalability</strong> is how well it keeps performing as load grows: <em>scaling up</em> means a bigger machine, <em>scaling out</em> means more machines sharing the work. Bottlenecks move: when the web servers are fixed, the database becomes the limit. Ask: what is the current bottleneck, what load do we expect, and what is the plan if volume doubles?</p>\n<p><strong>Availability.</strong> <strong>Availability</strong> is the share of time a service works as intended. Targets are stated in &quot;nines&quot;: 99.9% allows about 43.8 minutes of downtime in a month; 99.99% about 4.4 minutes; 99% about 7.3 hours. Each extra nine costs far more than the last, so the right target depends on what an outage costs the users and the business. <strong>Dependencies multiply:</strong> a feature that needs three services in a chain, each 99.9% available, is available only 0.999³ = 99.7% of the time, about 131 minutes of downtime a month. Reliability is a product decision as well as an engineering one.</p>\n<p><strong>Service level objectives.</strong> A <strong>service level objective</strong> (SLO) is a target for a measured aspect of service quality over a period, such as &quot;99.9% of requests succeed within 500 milliseconds, measured monthly&quot;. The measure is a <em>service level indicator</em> (SLI), a promise to customers is a <em>service level agreement</em> (SLA, with consequences if missed), and the allowed shortfall is the <em>error budget</em>. A 99.9% monthly objective gives an error budget of 43.8 minutes. If incidents have used 30 of them by mid-month, 13.8 minutes remain, and the team may slow risky launches until the budget recovers. This turns &quot;how reliable must we be?&quot; into a number that product and engineering can plan around (the practice is described publicly in Google's site reliability engineering materials <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Google SRE book]</code>).</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Numbers make reliability a tradeoff you can discuss. <strong>When not to chase nines.</strong> For an internal tool or an experiment, a lower target saves cost; spend reliability effort where an outage hurts users.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-c21-8-security-and-privacy-basics-including-on-device-versus\"></span>\n<h4>C21.8 Security and privacy basics, including on-device versus cloud processing</h4>\n<p><strong>Security basics a PM should know.</strong> <em>Authentication</em> verifies who someone is (a password, a code, a biometric); <em>authorization</em> decides what that person may do. Data should be protected <em>in transit</em> (encrypted across the network) and <em>at rest</em> (encrypted where stored). Apply the principle of <em>least privilege</em> (give each person and system only the access needed). Keep secrets (keys, passwords) out of code. Plan for abuse as well as misuse (C22): who might try to misuse this feature, and how? Security review belongs early in design (C16.7), when changes are cheap.</p>\n<p><strong>Privacy basics.</strong> Collect only the data you need (<em>data minimization</em>), say why (<em>purpose limitation</em>), keep it no longer than needed, let people see and delete their data where the law gives that right, and design so that a breach exposes as little as possible. Privacy laws differ by place and change: an overview as of 2026-10 <code>[as of 2026-10]</code> includes the European Union's General Data Protection Regulation (in force since 2018), California's consumer privacy law and its later amendments, and India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act of 2023, with its rules notified on 13 November 2025 and the main obligations applying from 13 May 2027 <code>[V: law-firm summaries of the DPDP Rules]</code>. These are not legal advice; involve legal and privacy specialists early (C16.7).</p>\n<p><strong>On-device versus cloud processing.</strong> <strong>On-device processing</strong> is running computation on the user's own device instead of sending data to a server. The choice is among latency, privacy, capability, cost, battery, offline use and speed of improvement (FW-C21-07):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Factor</th>\n<th>On-device</th>\n<th>Cloud</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Latency</td>\n<td>No network wait; limited by the device's speed</td>\n<td>Fast compute, plus network round trip, which varies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Privacy</td>\n<td>Data stays on the device</td>\n<td>Data is sent to and handled by a server</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Capability</td>\n<td>Limited by device memory and power; smaller models</td>\n<td>Larger, more capable models and more data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cost to the company</td>\n<td>Little per request; shifts compute to the user's device</td>\n<td>A cost per request that grows with usage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Offline use</td>\n<td>Works</td>\n<td>Fails without a connection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Battery</td>\n<td>Uses the device's battery</td>\n<td>Uses the radio and less compute</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Updates</td>\n<td>Tied to app or system releases</td>\n<td>Improved instantly on the server</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A common design is <em>hybrid</em>: handle most requests on the device and send only the hard ones to the cloud, with a clear explanation to users of what leaves the device. Worked example 4 computes the latency, quality and cost of the three choices for a voice assistant.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-c21-9-mobile-versus-web-platforms\"></span>\n<h4>C21.9 Mobile versus web platforms</h4>\n<p>A <em>web</em> product runs in a browser and updates the moment the server changes: every user gets the new version on their next load. A <em>native mobile</em> app is installed from a store, updates only when users update (so several versions are in use at once), and can use device features (camera, sensors, notifications) more fully. Consequences for a PM:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Release speed and fixes.</em> A web bug can be fixed in hours; a native bug may take days to ship and weeks to reach most users. Server-controlled behavior (flags, remote configuration) lets a native app change without a new release (C17.2).</li>\n<li><em>Old versions.</em> A native API must keep supporting old app versions (C19.3), since some users never update.</li>\n<li><em>Store rules.</em> Native apps are reviewed and governed by the store's policies (C19.4).</li>\n<li><em>Offline and connectivity.</em> Native apps can store data and work offline; web apps can too, with more effort.</li>\n<li><em>Notifications and engagement.</em> Push notifications are strong for re-engagement and easy to overuse (C10.9).</li>\n<li><em>Cross-platform tools</em> let one codebase serve several platforms, trading some native feel and performance for cost.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Regional Lens: India.</strong> In markets where many people use lower-priced Android phones on variable mobile networks, page weight, memory use and offline behavior decide whether a product works at all <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: author's synthesis; no source checked]</code>. Technology companies have published lightweight versions of apps for such conditions <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Android Go apps (Google Go, Maps Go and YouTube Go, which was retired in 2022) and Facebook Lite, 2017 coverage]</code>. The consequence for a PM: set a performance budget (for example, the app must work on a phone with limited memory and on a slow connection), test on representative devices and networks and not on the team's own phones, and treat data cost as a user cost (C09). The numbers vary widely by region and over time; check current device and network data before designing.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c21-c21-10-technical-debt-build-versus-buy-versus-partner\"></span>\n<h4>C21.10 Technical debt; build versus buy versus partner</h4>\n<p>C16.4 defined technical debt and showed how to discuss it as an investment with a payback; C18.7 gave the build-buy-partner chooser. The technical lens adds three questions. <em>For debt:</em> what part of the system is affected, and what happens if it fails or cannot change (a fragile billing module is a larger risk than a fragile reporting script)? <em>For build:</em> what must we maintain, secure and scale afterwards, and do we have people who can? <em>For buy or partner:</em> what is the interface (an API), how will we depend on it, what happens if the vendor changes terms, is down or goes away, and how hard is it to leave (a switching cost, C18.5)? Technical fluency helps you ask whether an integration is a one-day task (a well-documented API) or a quarter's work (a custom data migration).</p>\n<span id=\"c21-c21-11-estimating-engineering-effort-reading-design-document\"></span>\n<h4>C21.11 Estimating engineering effort; reading design documents</h4>\n<p>You do not estimate effort for engineers, but you can anticipate what drives it (FW-C21-08). Effort rises with: a <em>new data model or a migration</em> of existing data, <em>external dependencies</em> (another team's work, a vendor), <em>security or privacy review</em>, <em>scale</em> requirements, support for <em>several platforms or old versions</em>, <em>unknowns</em> (a technology the team has not used), and <em>quality bar</em> (accessibility, reliability). Ask engineers for a range with assumptions, and the riskiest part (C16.4).</p>\n<p><strong>Reading a design document.</strong> Engineers write design documents before big work. Read them for: the goal and non-goals (do they match the product goal?), the alternatives considered and why one was chosen, the data model and API (the contracts), the failure modes and what the user sees when something fails, how it will be rolled out and rolled back, the cost to run, and open questions. Ask one or two focused questions rather than many: &quot;What happens to the user if the queue backs up?&quot; &quot;What is the plan if the migration fails halfway?&quot; A PM who reads design documents carefully earns trust and catches product risks early.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Eight frameworks, set out below. The estimate conversation (FW-C16-04), the technical-debt conversation (FW-C16-05), the build-buy-partner chooser (FW-C18-07), the developer-experience funnel (FW-C19-04), the rollout ladder (FW-C17-02) and the percentile guidance in C12 are reused, not repeated.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c21-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C21-01 · The request-path map · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a user action and the system that serves it.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the hops from client to data store and back, with for each hop what it does, what can fail, its share of the latency, and who owns it.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the main path is known or can be learned from engineers.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a simple map hides detail and finds the right hop.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> treating &quot;the server&quot; as one box; ignoring name lookup, network and the device; averages instead of percentiles.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 1 and CS-C21-02.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Hop</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>What can fail</th>\n<th>User sees</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c21-02\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C21-02 · The engineer conversation questions · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a proposed feature or a design document.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> ten questions that surface cost, risk and user impact.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> engineers welcome focused questions asked early.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> many questions feel like an audit; pick the two that matter most.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> asking to be impressive; accepting &quot;it's complicated&quot;; telling engineers how to build.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> IQ-C21-04 and IQ-C21-07.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>What is the riskiest part, and what would reduce the risk? 2. What changes in the data model? 3. What does this depend on outside our team? 4. What happens to the user when it fails? 5. What happens if the request is sent twice? 6. How fresh does the data need to be? 7. What does it cost to run at expected volume? 8. What happens at ten times the load? 9. How will we roll it out and roll it back? 10. What are we not building, and what will that cost later?</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"fw-c21-03\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C21-03 · The latency budget · Original</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a user-visible action and a target response time.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a budget split across hops, the measured percentiles against it, and the first hop to fix.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> timings can be measured per hop.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> optimizing a small hop wastes effort; the largest contributor to the tail is the one to attack.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> averages; optimizing without measuring; ignoring the slow device and network.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 1.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: <strong>Set the target at a percentile → List the hops → Measure each at that percentile → Find the largest contributor → Fix it → Re-measure.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c21-04\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C21-04 · The SQL read-and-reason checklist · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a query and its result.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> answers to six checks (grain, join behavior, filters, null handling, time zone, total against a known number) and a verdict on whether to trust the result.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can inspect a small sample of the underlying rows.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> the check takes minutes and prevents wrong decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> summing across a fan-out; filtering out nulls silently; mixing time zones; trusting an unchecked total.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 2.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Check</th>\n<th>Question</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Grain</td>\n<td>What is one row of each table?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Join</td>\n<td>Does it repeat rows (fan-out) or drop them?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Filter</td>\n<td>What is excluded, and is that intended?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nulls</td>\n<td>How are missing values handled?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time</td>\n<td>Which time zone and what date boundary?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>Does it match a number I already trust?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c21-05\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C21-05 · The architecture tradeoff table · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a design pattern under discussion (services, cache, queue, synchronous or asynchronous, replica).</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> for each pattern what it buys, what it costs, and the question a PM should ask.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the problem it is meant to solve is stated.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> every pattern adds parts; simplicity is a feature.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> adopting a pattern for fashion; ignoring staleness and failure handling.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 3.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Buys</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n<th>Ask</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Services</td>\n<td>Independent teams and scaling</td>\n<td>Network calls; coordination</td>\n<td>Who owns each side?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cache</td>\n<td>Speed; lower load</td>\n<td>Staleness; invalidation</td>\n<td>How fresh must it be?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Queue</td>\n<td>Responsiveness; burst absorption</td>\n<td>Delay; retries; ordering</td>\n<td>What does the user see while waiting?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Replicas</td>\n<td>Read capacity; resilience</td>\n<td>Copies disagree briefly</td>\n<td>Can the user see stale data?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c21-06\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C21-06 · The reliability targets sheet · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a service, what an outage costs and its dependencies.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> an availability target in nines with the downtime it allows, the compounded availability of the dependency chain, an SLO with its error budget, and a policy for what happens when the budget is spent.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the cost of downtime can be estimated.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> each nine costs more than the last.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a target no one measures; ignoring dependency compounding; no policy for the budget.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 5.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Formulas: <strong>downtime per month = (1 − availability) × 43,800 minutes; chain availability = product of the availabilities; error budget = allowed downtime.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c21-07\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C21-07 · The on-device versus cloud chooser · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a feature that processes user data.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a comparison on latency, privacy, capability, cost, offline use, battery and update speed, a hybrid routing rule if suitable, and what the user is told.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the numbers for compute, network and cost can be estimated.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> privacy and offline use favor the device; capability and update speed favor the cloud.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> deciding on one factor; ignoring the poor-network case; no disclosure to users.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 4 and IQ-C21-03.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"fw-c21-08\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C21-08 · Effort drivers and the design-document read · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a proposed project or a design document.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the effort drivers present (new data model or migration, external dependencies, security or privacy review, scale, platform and version coverage, unknowns, quality bar), the riskiest part, and a list of questions from the read.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> engineers will give a range with assumptions.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> reading takes time and finds risks early.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> reading only the summary; asking many questions; accepting a single date.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> IQ-C21-07 and EX-C21-08.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c21-4-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>4. Answer architecture</h3>\n<span id=\"c21-the-technical-fluency-answer-for-a-20-minute-round\"></span>\n<h4>The technical-fluency answer, for a 20-minute round</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Minutes</th>\n<th>What to produce</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Clarify the scenario</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>What the feature does, for whom, what matters (speed, privacy, cost, reliability)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Map the path or components</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>The request path or the system parts; where time, data and failure concentrate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Identify the constraint</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>The binding constraint with a number (latency target, volume, availability, privacy rule)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Options and tradeoffs</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Two or three options with what each buys and costs, using the tradeoff tables</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recommendation and risks</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>The choice, the riskiest part, the questions you would ask engineers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Measures</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>How you would know it worked: percentile latency, error rate, availability, cost per request</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Pacing rules.</strong> For an &quot;explain X&quot; question, give the plain definition, an everyday analogy, an example from a product, and one tradeoff, in 90 seconds, then stop. If you run behind in a scenario, keep the path map, the constraint with its number and the recommendation.</p>\n<p><strong>How to say it.</strong> Be plain and correct: &quot;A cache keeps a copy near the user so it's faster; the price is that the copy can be out of date, so I'd ask how fresh this data has to be.&quot; Say what you do not know and how you would find out.</p>\n<p><strong>How to close.</strong> Two sentences: &quot;I'd choose [option] because [constraint with a number], accepting [cost]. I'd ask engineering about [riskiest part], and I'd watch [percentile or error measure] to know it works.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c21-type-explain-a-concept-api-cache-database-latency\"></span>\n<h4>Type: explain a concept (API, cache, database, latency)</h4>\n<p>Plain definition, analogy, product example, tradeoff, in about 90 seconds.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-type-what-happens-when-a-user-does-x\"></span>\n<h4>Type: what happens when a user does X</h4>\n<p>Walk the request path hop by hop; say where time and failures concentrate.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-type-a-performance-problem\"></span>\n<h4>Type: a performance problem</h4>\n<p>Use the latency budget: percentile, hops, largest contributor, fix, re-measure.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-type-an-architecture-or-design-choice\"></span>\n<h4>Type: an architecture or design choice</h4>\n<p>Use the tradeoff table; ask what the choice buys and costs and who owns each side.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-type-on-device-versus-cloud-privacy-latency-cost\"></span>\n<h4>Type: on-device versus cloud (privacy, latency, cost)</h4>\n<p>Use the chooser, compute a number for each side, and consider a hybrid.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-type-reliability-or-a-launch-with-risk\"></span>\n<h4>Type: reliability or a launch with risk</h4>\n<p>Use the reliability sheet and the launch tools from C17.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-common-interviewer-interventions-and-what-to-do\"></span>\n<h4>Common interviewer interventions, and what to do</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Interviewer says</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What happens when I tap this?&quot;</td>\n<td>Request path</td>\n<td>Walk the hops; name where it can fail</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Why is it slow?&quot;</td>\n<td>Diagnosis</td>\n<td>Ask for percentiles; split by hop</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Can't we just cache it?&quot;</td>\n<td>Staleness</td>\n<td>Ask how fresh the data must be</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What if the request is sent twice?&quot;</td>\n<td>Idempotency</td>\n<td>Describe a key that makes repeats safe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Where does that number come from?&quot;</td>\n<td>Pipeline</td>\n<td>Ask about source, freshness and transformation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;How reliable does it need to be?&quot;</td>\n<td>Availability</td>\n<td>Give a target, the downtime it allows and the cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;I don't know&quot;</td>\n<td>Your edge</td>\n<td>Say what you would ask, and to whom</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c21-5-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h3>5. Worked examples</h3>\n<p>All five examples are <code>Illustrative</code>: the products are fictional or use a product type as the setting only, and every figure is an invented assumption. The arithmetic was computed. Each is written as notes you could say aloud.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-worked-example-1-where-does-the-time-go-a-product-page-s-req\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 1 — Where does the time go? A product page's request path (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A shopping app's product page takes about 2.3 seconds at the median and 5.2 seconds at the 95th percentile, and the team's target is a 95th percentile under 3 seconds. The PM asks engineering for the timing of each hop (FW-C21-01 and FW-C21-03).</p>\n<p><strong>Median timeline (milliseconds, invented).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Hop</th>\n<th>Median</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Name lookup (DNS)</td>\n<td>60</td>\n<td>Finding the server's address</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Connection setup and security handshake</td>\n<td>180</td>\n<td>Opening a secure connection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Server compute</td>\n<td>300</td>\n<td>Application logic and calls to other services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Database</td>\n<td>420</td>\n<td>The product and price queries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transfer (600 KB over 6 Mbps)</td>\n<td>800</td>\n<td>Sending the page and images (600 × 8 ÷ 6,000 seconds)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rendering and scripts on the device</td>\n<td>500</td>\n<td>Drawing the page</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Total</strong></td>\n<td><strong>2,260</strong></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>The slow tail.</strong> At the 95th percentile (invented): lookup 120, connection 300, server 500, database 2,000, transfer 1,400 (slow networks), rendering 900: total 5,220 ms. The database is the largest contributor in the tail (2,000 of 5,220), though it is only the third largest at the median. An average would have hidden this.</p>\n<p><strong>Fixes, in order of effect on the tail.</strong> (1) The database query scans a large table; an index on the lookup column (C21.4) cuts the tail from 2,000 to about 180 ms. (2) Images are uncompressed; compressing them halves the bytes, cutting the tail transfer from 1,400 to about 700 ms. (3) A cache for the product details (C21.5) cuts server compute in the tail from 500 to about 300 ms. After the fixes the tail is 120 + 300 + 300 + 180 + 700 + 900 = 2,500 ms, under the 3-second target. The median becomes 60 + 180 + 150 + 60 + 400 + 500 = 1,350 ms.</p>\n<p><strong>What not to do first.</strong> Rendering at 900 ms in the tail is real but a smaller piece; start with the largest contributor.</p>\n<p><strong>Check for side effects.</strong> The cache can serve stale prices (set a short lifetime, and bypass the cache at checkout); the index slows writes slightly; the image compression must not harm quality. Measure the percentile again after each change (re-measure is the last step in FW-C21-03).</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Splitting the time by hop at the percentile that matters pointed to the database, a fix that an average-based look and a generic &quot;make it faster&quot; would have missed.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-worked-example-2-reading-a-sql-query-and-catching-a-fan-out-\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 2 — Reading a SQL query and catching a fan-out (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> An analyst reports that revenue by country last week was $140 for the sample below, but finance says $100. The PM reads the query (FW-C21-04). This is read-and-reason work; no one is asked to write SQL.</p>\n<p><strong>The data, a tiny sample (invented).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>orders</code>: order 1, user u1, $40; order 2, user u2, $25; order 3, user u1, $35.</li>\n<li><code>order_items</code>: order 1 has items A and B; order 2 has item C; order 3 has item D.</li>\n<li><code>users</code>: u1 is in India (IN); u2 is in the United States (US).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The query.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>SELECT u.country, SUM(o.amount) AS revenue\nFROM orders o\nJOIN order_items i ON i.order_id = o.order_id\nJOIN users u ON u.user_id = o.user_id\nGROUP BY u.country\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Read it with the checklist.</strong> <em>Grain:</em> one row of <code>orders</code> is an order; one row of <code>order_items</code> is an item; many items can belong to one order. <em>Join:</em> joining orders to items produces one row per item, so order 1 now appears twice. That is a fan-out. <em>Aggregation:</em> <code>SUM(o.amount)</code> then adds order 1's $40 twice.</p>\n<p><strong>Compute it.</strong> After the join: IN has rows for order 1 (twice, $40 each) and order 3 ($35): $40 + $40 + $35 = $115. US has order 2 ($25). The query reports $115 + $25 = $140. The true figures are IN $40 + $35 = $75 and US $25, total $100. The query overstates revenue by 40%, which explains the discrepancy exactly.</p>\n<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Sum revenue at the order grain without joining items (first aggregate orders by user, or leave out the items table, which the question does not need). If items are needed, count orders with a distinct order count or aggregate items separately.</p>\n<p><strong>Other checks.</strong> Is each date in the same time zone as finance's? Are orders with a missing user dropped by the join (and is that intended)? Does the total now match the number finance trusts ($100)? Matching a known total is the strongest check.</p>\n<p><strong>What the PM does.</strong> Say, &quot;Revenue per order is at the order grain; the join to items repeats orders. Can we recompute without the items table and check it against finance's $100?&quot; This is a precise, non-accusatory question that points to the cause.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Knowing the grain and what a join does to row counts found a 40% error from the shape of the query alone.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-worked-example-3-a-cache-and-a-latency-budget-for-search-sug\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 3 — A cache and a latency budget for search suggestions (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a search-suggestion service of the kind Google runs. No fact about any real system is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> As users type, the product suggests completions and must respond within about 100 milliseconds to feel instant (C21.2). The backend takes about 220 ms to compute suggestions (invented). A cache can answer in about 10 ms when it has the query.</p>\n<p><strong>Average latency by hit rate.</strong> Average = hit rate × 10 + (1 − hit rate) × 220.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Hit rate</th>\n<th>Average latency</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>70%</td>\n<td>0.7 × 10 + 0.3 × 220 = 73 ms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>90%</td>\n<td>31 ms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>95%</td>\n<td>20.5 ms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>96%</td>\n<td>18.4 ms</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>The tail.</strong> The average improves smoothly, but the percentiles do not. At a 90% hit rate, 10% of requests miss, so the 95th percentile is the slow backend: 220 ms, above the target. Only when the hit rate exceeds 95% does the 95th percentile fall to the cache's 10 ms; the 99th percentile stays at 220 ms until the hit rate passes 99%. A team that looks only at the average will declare victory at 90% (31 ms) while one request in ten feels slow.</p>\n<p><strong>What decides the hit rate.</strong> Search queries are very unevenly distributed: a small set of popular queries accounts for most traffic. Suppose (invented) that the most popular million queries cover 90% of requests; caching them costs a modest amount of memory. Covering the next 5% might need ten times as many entries, so there is a point of diminishing returns. The cost of memory is a number to compare with the value of the faster tail.</p>\n<p><strong>Freshness.</strong> Suggestions that reflect what is trending right now go stale in a cache. A lifetime of 5 minutes means a surge in a new query appears in suggestions up to 5 minutes late. The product must decide how fresh suggestions must be: for a breaking-news event, a short lifetime or a faster path for trending queries; for the long tail of ordinary queries, a long lifetime is fine.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Cache the popular queries with a lifetime of minutes, send trending queries through a short-lifetime path, set the target as a 95th-percentile latency under 100 ms (not the average), and monitor hit rate and the percentile together. Ask engineering: what is the hit rate at the planned cache size, and what happens to the user when the backend is slow? (Show cached suggestions, then update.)</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The average hid the tail, the hit rate drove the speed and the cost, and freshness was the product decision.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-worked-example-4-on-device-or-cloud-for-a-voice-assistant-il\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 4 — On-device or cloud for a voice assistant (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a voice assistant on a phone of the kind Apple and several other companies make. No fact about any real product is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The question.</strong> Should voice requests be processed on the device, in the cloud, or both? (FW-C21-07.)</p>\n<p><strong>Numbers (invented).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>On-device</th>\n<th>Cloud</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Compute time</td>\n<td>150 ms</td>\n<td>40 ms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Network (good connection)</td>\n<td>none</td>\n<td>60 ms round trip + 20 ms upload</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Network (poor connection)</td>\n<td>none</td>\n<td>400 ms round trip + 20 ms upload</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Offline</td>\n<td>works</td>\n<td>fails</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Accuracy on easy requests (80% of traffic)</td>\n<td>95%</td>\n<td>96%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Accuracy on hard requests (20% of traffic)</td>\n<td>70%</td>\n<td>90%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cost to the company per request</td>\n<td>about 0</td>\n<td>$0.002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data leaving the device</td>\n<td>none</td>\n<td>the audio or transcript</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Latency.</strong> On-device: 150 ms always. Cloud on a good connection: 40 + 60 + 20 = 120 ms. Cloud on a poor connection: 40 + 400 + 20 = 460 ms, and nothing when offline. So the cloud is a little faster on a good connection and much slower on a poor one. On variable networks, on-device is the more predictable choice.</p>\n<p><strong>Quality.</strong> On-device only: 0.8 × 95% + 0.2 × 70% = 90%. Cloud only: 0.8 × 96% + 0.2 × 90% = 94.8%. The cloud's advantage is on hard requests.</p>\n<p><strong>Hybrid.</strong> Handle requests on the device when its confidence is high (the 80% of easy requests) and send the hard 20% to the cloud, with a routing step that takes about 10 ms. Average latency: 0.8 × 150 + 0.2 × (10 + 120) = 120 + 26 = 146 ms. Quality: easy requests on the device at 95% and hard requests in the cloud at 90%: 0.8 × 95% + 0.2 × 90% = 94%, within one point of cloud-only (94.8%) and four points above on-device only.</p>\n<p><strong>Cost.</strong> At 1 billion requests a day, all-cloud costs 1,000,000,000 × $0.002 = $2.0 million a day; the hybrid sends 20% to the cloud: $0.4 million a day. The on-device option moves the compute cost to users' devices (battery and memory).</p>\n<p><strong>Privacy.</strong> On-device keeps all data local; the hybrid sends only the hard requests, and the product should say so plainly and let users control it. For sensitive categories (health, messages) the routing rule can keep requests on the device regardless of difficulty.</p>\n<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> Hybrid: on-device first, cloud for hard requests, with disclosure and a user setting, a rule that keeps sensitive categories local, and a measure of the share routed to the cloud. Ask engineering: how much memory and battery does the on-device model use, how often will the model be updated, and what happens when the cloud is slow (fall back to the on-device answer)? What would change my view: on-device quality closing the gap (move more on-device), or the cloud cost falling.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The arithmetic made the tradeoff concrete across latency, quality, cost and privacy, and the hybrid captured most of the benefit of each.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-worked-example-5-reliability-targets-for-a-payments-feature-\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 5 — Reliability targets for a payments feature (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A team is adding a payment step to its app and asks what reliability to promise. The step depends on three services in a chain: the app's own service, a fraud-check service and the payment partner, each currently about 99.9% available (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>What nines mean (FW-C21-06).</strong> A month has about 43,834 minutes (30.44 days). 99.9% availability allows 0.001 × 43,834 = 43.8 minutes of downtime; 99.99% allows 4.4 minutes; 99% allows 438 minutes (over 7 hours).</p>\n<p><strong>The chain.</strong> Three services in series, each 99.9%: 0.999³ = 0.997, so about 99.7% available, or 0.003 × 43,834 = 131 minutes of downtime a month, three times the budget of a single 99.9% service. Five services at 99.95% each give 0.9995⁵ = 99.75%, about 110 minutes. The feature's availability is lower than any one component's.</p>\n<p><strong>Choosing a target.</strong> What does an outage cost? Suppose payments bring in $1,500 a minute at peak and average $600 a minute (invented): 131 minutes of downtime a month costs about $79,000 in lost sales (plus trust). Moving from 99.7% to 99.9% (43.8 minutes) would save about 87 minutes, or $52,000 a month. If the engineering cost of the extra redundancy is less than that, it pays. The answer depends on the numbers, and the team should set the target by comparing cost and benefit.</p>\n<p><strong>An SLO and its budget.</strong> The team sets an objective: 99.9% of payment attempts succeed (excluding customer errors such as declined cards), measured monthly. The error budget is 43.8 minutes of equivalent failure. Mid-month, incidents have used 30 minutes, leaving 13.8. A policy agreed in advance: when less than 25% of the budget remains (below 10.96 minutes), pause risky launches and focus on reliability until the budget recovers. At 13.8 minutes the team may continue but with caution.</p>\n<p><strong>Idempotency and retries.</strong> Because a payment must not be charged twice when a request is retried, the payment call carries an idempotency key (C21.3). This is a reliability feature as well as a correctness one: it allows safe retries, which raises the effective success rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Reducing the chain.</strong> Ask what is on the critical path. Can the fraud check be asynchronous for low-risk transactions (C21.5)? Can the payment partner be backed up by a second provider? Each removal raises availability more than hardening a single component.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Compounded availability explained why the feature was less reliable than its parts, the cost arithmetic set the target, and the error budget turned reliability into a planning tool.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-6-baseline-strong-standout\"></span>\n<h3>6. Baseline, Strong, Standout</h3>\n<p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;An analyst says weekly revenue by country was $140,000, but finance says $100,000. How would you work out the discrepancy?&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c21-baseline-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Baseline answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I'd ask the analyst and finance to get together and agree on one number, or check which one is right. Probably the analyst made a mistake, so I'd ask them to redo the query.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>What is wrong:</em> no method, assumes blame, and does not locate the cause. It would not find a systematic error.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-strong-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Strong answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;First I'd confirm they mean the same thing: same date range, same time zone, same definition of revenue (before or after refunds), same countries. If those match, the difference is likely in the query. I'd ask to see it and check what each table holds and how they are joined.</p>\n<p>&quot;A common cause of a number that is too high is a join that repeats rows: joining orders to order items counts each order once per item. I'd test it by checking whether the query's row count exceeds the number of orders, and by comparing a single order's total against its rows.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c21-standout-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Standout answer</h4>\n<p>Everything in the Strong answer, plus:</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd use the 40% gap as a clue: $140,000 against $100,000 is 1.4 times, which suggests that some orders are being counted about twice on average, consistent with a join to a table with more than one row per order. I'd reproduce it on a tiny sample, say three orders, where I can compute the right answer by hand: if one order with two items gives its amount twice, the query has fanned out.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd fix it by summing at the order grain before any join to items, and then check that the result matches the number finance trusts. I'd also check other usual suspects in order: time zone and date boundaries, orders with missing users dropped by the join, test orders, refunds and currency.</p>\n<p>&quot;The deeper fix is to prevent it happening again: agree one definition of revenue with finance and put it in a single shared, tested metric, with a reconciliation check against finance each week. I'd say this to the analyst as a shared process improvement, not a mistake.</p>\n<p>&quot;What I would not do: pick a number because it looks plausible, or assume which side is wrong before checking.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c21-what-moves-an-answer-up-a-band\"></span>\n<h4>What moves an answer up a band</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>From Baseline to Strong</th>\n<th>From Strong to Standout</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Confirms definitions and looks at the query</td>\n<td>Names the fan-out as a likely cause and tests it on a tiny sample</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Checks the join and row counts</td>\n<td>Reconciles to a trusted number and checks time zone, nulls and test data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Is neutral about who is wrong</td>\n<td>Proposes a shared definition and an ongoing reconciliation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c21-7-company-lens\"></span>\n<h3>7. Company lens</h3>\n<p>All claims describe what companies say publicly or what candidates commonly report, not insider knowledge. Interview formats change <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Apple [as of 2026-10].</strong> Apple has described doing a large share of intelligence features on the device and has described a design for processing in the cloud with privacy protections for requests that need more capacity <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Apple privacy page: on-device processing with Private Cloud Compute]</code>. Third-party guides report that technical-fluency prompts at Apple concern on-device versus cloud processing, privacy and latency <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: compare the two on latency, privacy, capability and cost, propose a hybrid with a clear disclosure, and state what stays on the device.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Google [as of 2026-10].</strong> Google has published its approach to site reliability, including service level objectives and error budgets, and its engineering is known for work on latency at very large scale <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Google SRE book, 2016, and sre.google]</code>. Candidates commonly report scale and performance prompts <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: use percentiles, state an objective with a budget, and explain how a cache changes the tail.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Meta [as of 2026-10].</strong> Meta has published an account of its October 2021 outage, which began with a change to its backbone network <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Meta engineering post of 5 October 2021]</code> (section 10). Candidates commonly report prompts on reliability, rollout and what happens when a safety mechanism misfires <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: discuss blast radius, independence of recovery tools, and drills.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Microsoft [as of 2026-10].</strong> Microsoft operates cloud services and enterprise products where reliability, compliance and administrator control matter, and publishes incident reviews <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Azure status history publishes post incident reviews]</code>. Candidates commonly report prompts on security, compliance and service reliability in enterprise settings <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: cover authentication and authorization, audit logs, data residency and availability commitments.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Amazon [as of 2026-10].</strong> Amazon runs a cloud platform and an online store where small increases in latency have been described as costly, and it emphasizes frugality <code>Public</code> <code>[V: AWS launch account; Frugality is a Leadership Principle; Greg Linden, Make Data Useful, Stanford talk slides, 29 November 2006, for the latency claim]</code>. Candidates commonly report prompts that weigh speed, cost per request and reliability <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: state cost per request at expected volume, and use measured effects of latency, not a remembered statistic.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Netflix [as of 2026-10].</strong> Netflix has published on running a large service on a cloud provider and on testing resilience by injecting failures <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Netflix Tech Blog posts on experimentation and resilience testing]</code>. Candidates commonly report prompts on reliability and graceful degradation <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: describe what the user sees when a dependency fails (a fallback, not a blank screen), and how you would test it.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Stripe [as of 2026-10].</strong> Stripe's API documentation describes idempotency keys so that retried requests do not repeat an action, and the company is widely described as emphasizing API quality <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Stripe API documentation on idempotency, webhooks and versioning]</code>. Candidates report prompts on API design and reliability <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: raise idempotency, versioning and clear errors whenever money or irreversible actions are involved.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c21-8-common-mistakes\"></span>\n<h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Mistake</th>\n<th>How to detect it</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Jargon used incorrectly</td>\n<td>A term you cannot explain simply</td>\n<td>Use plain words; say when you are unsure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Averages instead of percentiles</td>\n<td>&quot;The average is fine&quot;</td>\n<td>Ask for the 95th and 99th percentile</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>&quot;Just cache it&quot;</td>\n<td>No thought of staleness</td>\n<td>Ask how fresh the data must be</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>No idempotency on actions</td>\n<td>Retries could repeat a charge or message</td>\n<td>Ask what happens if it is sent twice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Trusting a joined total</td>\n<td>A number that exceeds a known figure</td>\n<td>Check the grain and the join; match a known total</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Ignoring dependency compounding</td>\n<td>Each service &quot;is 99.9%&quot;</td>\n<td>Multiply the chain; shorten it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>One-factor on-device or cloud choice</td>\n<td>&quot;Cloud is faster&quot;</td>\n<td>Compare latency, privacy, cost, capability, offline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Ignoring the poor-network and old-device case</td>\n<td>Tested only on the team's phones</td>\n<td>Test on representative devices and networks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Telling engineers how to build</td>\n<td>&quot;Use a queue&quot;</td>\n<td>State what must be true; ask for options</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Ignoring cost per request</td>\n<td>Cost to build only</td>\n<td>Ask for cost at expected volume</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c21-9-mental-models-applied\"></span>\n<h3>9. Mental models applied</h3>\n<p>Four models, each tied to a decision in this chapter. <a href=\"#gl-second-order-effect\">second-order effect</a> is from C02.</p>\n<p><strong>Amdahl's law</strong> (<code>MM-amdahl</code>). The overall speedup from improving one part of a process is limited by the share of the whole that part represents <code>[V: Amdahl's law, a mathematical identity]</code>. If a hop is 5% of the time, making it free gains at most 5%. Use it to choose what to fix: find the largest contributor at the percentile that matters (Worked example 1).</p>\n<p><strong>Single point of failure</strong> (<code>MM-spof</code>). A component whose failure stops the whole system. Look for one in every request path and every recovery path. CS-C21-02 shows a single point that was hidden: the system's own recovery tools depended on the network that had failed.</p>\n<p><strong>Leaky abstractions</strong> (<code>MM-leaky-abstractions</code>). Simplifying layers hide detail until the detail matters; a simple API hides a network that can fail, a database that can be slow, a cache that can be stale <code>[V: Spolsky, The Law of Leaky Abstractions, 2002]</code>. A PM who knows the layer beneath the one they use is less surprised.</p>\n<p><strong>The fallacies of distributed computing</strong> (<code>MM-fallacies</code>). A list of false assumptions engineers make about networked systems: that the network is reliable, that latency is zero, that bandwidth is infinite, that the network is secure <code>[V: Fallacies of distributed computing (eight assumptions), as listed in software-engineering law references]</code>. Each is a prompt for a product question: what does the user see when the network is slow, or down?</p>\n<span id=\"c21-engineering-design-and-data-science-perspectives\"></span>\n<h3>Engineering, design and data-science perspectives</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engineering.</strong> Engineers want product requirements that state what must be true (latency target, freshness, availability) and leave the how to them. Ask the ten questions in FW-C21-02; thank them for risks surfaced early; avoid specifying a solution in the spec.</li>\n<li><strong>Design and research.</strong> Designers care about what the user sees while the system is working or failing: loading, error and offline states (C10.5). Share the latency budget with them so that perceived speed (progress, skeleton screens) is designed in.</li>\n<li><strong>Data science.</strong> Data scientists depend on pipelines and instrumentation. Agree the event definitions, the freshness needed and who owns each transformation, and ask for reconciliation checks against trusted totals.</li>\n<li><strong>Security, privacy and legal.</strong> They need to know what data is collected, where it is processed and who can see it. Bring them into the on-device versus cloud decision and the retention design before the build.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c21-checklist-before-you-commit-to-a-technical-approach\"></span>\n<h3>Checklist: before you commit to a technical approach</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Can I draw the request path and say where time and failure concentrate?</li>\n<li>Have I asked for percentiles, not averages?</li>\n<li>Do I know the contracts (API, schema) that others depend on, and what happens if a request is sent twice?</li>\n<li>Have I asked how fresh the data must be before accepting a cache or a copy?</li>\n<li>Do I know where each number comes from, and have I checked a joined total against a known figure?</li>\n<li>Have I compounded the availability of the dependency chain and set a target with a budget?</li>\n<li>For any feature that processes personal data, have I compared on-device and cloud and said what leaves the device?</li>\n<li>Have I asked engineering for the riskiest part, the cost at expected volume, and the rollback plan?</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c21-10-case-studies\"></span>\n<h3>10. Case studies</h3>\n<span id=\"cs-c21-01\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C21-01 · The search page that scanned two million rows · Illustrative · Beginner</h5>\n<p><em>Fictional composite. Every number is invented (invented).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> A marketplace's search page takes 3.1 seconds to load on average. Support hears that it is slow, and the team has proposed a full redesign of the front end. The PM asks engineering where the time goes before approving the project.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A small team, a quarter of capacity, and a belief that &quot;the page is heavy.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Engineers instrument the request path. Of the 3.1 seconds: name lookup and connection 0.3, transfer and rendering 0.5, application logic 0.2, and the database query that finds matching listings 2.1 seconds. The query filters a table of 2,000,000 listings by category and city, and the table has no index on those columns, so the database examines every row. A check of the query plan confirms a full table scan.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> As an observable problem: &quot;Most of the page's load time is one database query that reads every row because the lookup column has no index; the front end is a small part of the delay.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> A redesign of the front end could at best save part of the 0.5 seconds of transfer and rendering (about 16% of the page), while the query accounts for 68% (Amdahl's law). Adding an index on category and city lets the database find matching listings directly. Engineers estimate two days of work, plus a few hours to build the index on a large table without blocking writes, and a small cost in write time and storage. With the index, the query takes 12 milliseconds instead of 2,100 (a 175-fold improvement), and the page load falls to 0.3 + 0.5 + 0.2 + 0.012 = about 1.0 second, which engineers measure at 0.8 to 1.0 seconds.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Redesign the front end</td>\n<td>Look and feel; maybe 0.2 s</td>\n<td>A quarter of capacity; the main delay remains</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Add the index and fix other slow queries</td>\n<td>The largest contributor</td>\n<td>Two days; slight write cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Add a cache in front of the query</td>\n<td>Repeated searches</td>\n<td>Staleness; complexity; the first search stays slow</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B is the cheapest and fixes the biggest cause. C helps repeated searches and leaves the first one slow. A spends the most and changes the least.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Choose B, and measure percentiles before and after (the 95th percentile, not only the average). Primary measure: 95th-percentile page load time; guardrails: write latency on listing creation, and search result correctness. Because speed changes may affect orders for other reasons, run the change behind a flag with a holdback group to measure the effect on conversion (C14).</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> In this fictional scenario the 95th-percentile load time fell from 5.4 to 1.7 seconds, and the conversion rate on search sessions rose from 2.1% to 2.5% in the exposed group against a flat holdback (invented). On 1,000,000 search sessions a month, 0.4 points is 4,000 more orders, at an average of $30, about $120,000 a month. The redesign was deferred, and its scope was reduced to what testing showed to matter.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> If the indexed query had still been slow at the tail, the team would have looked at the second-largest contributor (transfer on slow networks) next. If conversion had not moved, the faster page would still be worth keeping, but the case for further speed work would weaken. The lesson: find which hop dominates before choosing the project.</p>\n<span id=\"cs-c21-02\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C21-02 · Facebook, October 2021: when the front door disappeared · Verified · Advanced</h5>\n<p><em>Sources (types): Meta's own engineering account of the outage, &quot;More details about the October 4 outage&quot; by Santosh Janardhan (5 October 2021), read in full (primary, for the cause and the design of the systems involved); Cloudflare's analysis of the same day, &quot;Understanding how Facebook disappeared from the Internet&quot; (4 October 2021), and ThousandEyes' outage analysis, read through excerpts, for the external timing; and trade-press reporting (The Register, ComputerWeekly) of Meta's post. The analysis is the author's. The sources do not reveal Meta's internal records. Re-read in October 2026.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> On 4 October 2021, from about 15:40 UTC, Facebook, Instagram, WhatsApp and related services became unreachable worldwide. Cloudflare saw Facebook's routes return from about 21:20 UTC, ThousandEyes measured the disruption until about 22:45 UTC, and press reports describe an outage of about six hours, so the length depends on what is measured <code>[V: Cloudflare; ThousandEyes; press]</code>. Meta's account says the outage was triggered by the system that manages its global backbone network capacity. During routine maintenance, a command intended to assess the availability of global backbone capacity unintentionally took down all the connections in the backbone, disconnecting Meta's data centers from one another and from the internet. Meta says its systems are designed to audit such commands, but a bug in the audit tool prevented it from properly stopping this one <code>[V: Meta's post]</code>. Meta's account adds that its smaller edge locations host DNS servers, which disable their routing advertisements if they cannot reach the data centers (a sign of an unhealthy network connection). With the whole backbone out, all of them withdrew those advertisements. The DNS servers remained operational but became unreachable, so the rest of the internet could not find Meta's servers.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A very large, tightly integrated network in which the systems used to diagnose and repair problems may depend on the same network that has failed. Meta says its data centers are designed with high levels of physical and system security, which slowed engineers who had to be sent on site, and that recovery had to bring load back gradually because dips in power use of tens of megawatts at individual data centers made a sudden surge risky <code>[V: Meta's post]</code>. Meta also says it had long run &quot;storm&quot; drills that take a service, data center or region offline, but had never run one that simulated its global backbone going offline <code>[V: Meta's post]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As reported: the date and approximate start; the trigger (a command during maintenance); the effect (all backbone connections down); the design of the DNS servers' health check (withdraw routes when unhealthy); the result (DNS unreachable though operational); a duration of about six hours; and a restoration that required engineers to go on site, because Meta says its primary and out-of-band network access was down and the loss of DNS broke many internal tools <code>[V: Meta's post]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> <em>The author's reading.</em> A change made to one system (backbone capacity management) had a global effect, and a safety mechanism in another system (DNS route withdrawal) turned a network outage into a total disappearance, while the tools to recover were affected by the same failure. As an observable problem: &quot;A command meant to be read-only for capacity took down the backbone, and everything that depended on it, including the way back, went with it.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> <em>The author's analysis.</em> The request path (FW-C21-01): a user's device asks DNS for the address of the service; DNS answers; the device connects across the internet to a data center. Meta's failure hit the first hop in an unusual way: not by crashing the DNS servers but by making them unreachable, because the routes to them were withdrawn. The user's experience was that the name did not resolve and nothing loaded.</p>\n<p><em>A safety mechanism as a single point of failure.</em> The health check made sense locally: a DNS server that cannot reach the data centers should not answer, because it might give outdated answers. At global scale the same rule meant that when every location lost its backend at once, every location removed itself. A rule that protects against a local failure can amplify a global one. The PM-level lesson is to ask, for every protective mechanism, what happens if it triggers everywhere at once.</p>\n";
